commit 1442a41d6acb0a21db9abd03bb975c585c6728ef
Author:     Kyle Gruel <kylegruel@gmail.com>
AuthorDate: Thu Aug 20 12:55:21 2026 -0500
Commit:     Kyle Gruel <kylegruel@gmail.com>
CommitDate: Thu Aug 20 12:55:21 2026 -0500

    fix(atoms): totalize refuses NaN key values loudly
    
    NaN compares false against everything including itself, so Python's sort
    neither raises nor defines a position for it: the resulting order depends on
    the input permutation. That contradicts totalize's one claim — that it
    totalizes — so a NaN key value now raises OrderingError naming the field and
    the value.
    
    Infinities remain ALLOWED. They order deterministically against every other
    float, so refusing them would overreach: the claim is totalization, not
    finiteness. The docstring says so.
    
    The check is float-typed only (math.isnan), and the strict type-identity check
    runs first, so a mixed-type key still refuses for the mixed-type reason and a
    string 'nan' is an ordinary str key.
    
    Refs: C-SOL-03 (sol r1, minor)
    
    Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01JpCUT3bF3dukDk5xjDejRM

