The current production prose is correct; the defect is in the claimed recurrence ratchet.

### C-SOL-07 — minor — [ordering.py](/Users/kaygee/Code/loops/libs/atoms/src/atoms/ordering.py:252)

**Claim:** NaN is rejected only in `K`, not in the id half of `(K, id)`. A float NaN returned by `get_id` silently restores permutation-dependent output instead of totalizing or refusing. This is the id-side analogue of C-SOL-03.

```text
('a', 'b', 'c') => ['a', 'b', 'c']
('c', 'b', 'a') => ['b', 'a', 'c']
('b', 'a', 'c') => ['b', 'a', 'c']
```

All records used equal `K=1.0`; only one tie-break id was NaN. This matters to the exported primitive and custom `get_id` callers, with the same bounded scope as the original in-memory NaN finding.

